Multiple LLM comparison. Claude Sonnet 4.6 (acc 0.89, Δπ=0.11) still shows 4.1× amplification. Table 17.\n**Appendix C**: Robustness to misspecification. Breakeven at 38% subgroup label noise. Table 18.\n**Appendix D**: Adaptive allocation. CATE-weighted achieves RMSE 0.020 vs uniform 0.027. Table 19.\n\n**Limitations** (0.25 pages): Requires a priori subgroup structure. K=8 with 50 labels fails. Computational overhead (~2.3s vs 0.008s). Binary/small-K only tested.\n\n**Ethics Statement**: Improves accuracy for marginalized subgroups. Notes potential misuse.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\n\nThe full experiment plan includes 11 experiments (exp-001 through exp-011). The paper covers ALL of them:\n- exp-001 (HB synthetic validation) → §5.2\n- exp-002 (Bias bound verification) → §5.1\n- exp-003 (Semi-synthetic political science) → §5.3\n- exp-004 (Real-data Ashwin et al.) → §5.4\n- exp-005 (Diagnostic ROC) → §3.3\n- exp-006 (Budget sensitivity) → Appendix A\n- exp-007 (PPCI orthogonality) → §5.5\n- exp-008 (Multiple LLM comparison) → Appendix B\n- exp-009 (Adaptive allocation) → Appendix D\n- exp-010 (Robustness to misspecification) → Appendix C\n- exp-011 (Causal forest integration) → §5.6\n\n## Your Review Tasks\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions and provide detailed analysis:\n\n### 1. Methodological Soundness\n- Is Theorem 1 (bias decomposition) correctly derived? Is the setup realistic?\n- Is the HB Dirichlet prior well-motivated? Are there alternatives that should be compared?\n- Is the diagnostic criterion (§3.3) well-justified statistically?\n- Are there any hidden assumptions that limit generalizability?\n\n### 2. Experimental Rigor\n- Are the synthetic experiments (§5.1-5.2) sufficient to validate the theory?\n- Is the semi-synthetic benchmark (§5.3) convincing? Is injecting known CATE structure into real text a valid approach?\n- Is the real-data validation (§5.4) strong enough given the limited sample sizes?\n- Are baselines