{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Bd8mzRFr5MZWGFg6cJQiA9","type":"tool_result","content":"0\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n1\t\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n2\t\\usepackage{times}\n3\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n9\t\\usepackage{enumitem}\n10\t\\usepackage{microtype}\n11\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n12\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n13\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n14\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n15\t\\usepackage[colorlinks,citecolor=blue,urlcolor=blue,linkcolor=blue]{hyperref}\n16\t\n17\t\\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}\n18\t\\newtheorem{proposition}{Proposition}\n19\t\\newtheorem{remark}{Remark}\n20\t\\newtheorem{definition}{Definition}\n21\t\n22\t\\newcommand{\\CATE}{\\mathrm{CATE}}\n23\t\\newcommand{\\bias}{\\mathrm{bias}}\n24\t\\newcommand{\\RMSE}{\\mathrm{RMSE}}\n25\t\n26\t\\title{When Debiasing Backfires: Measurement-Error Heterogeneity\\\\ in LLM-Annotated Causal Effect Estimation}\n27\t\n28\t\\author{Anonymous}\n29\t\n30\t\\begin{document}\n31\t\\maketitle\n32\t\n33\t\\begin{abstract}\n34\tLarge language models are increasingly used to annotate effect modifiers in heterogeneous treatment effect (HTE) analysis, yet their misclassification rates vary systematically across subgroups. We identify and formalize a previously unrecognized bias mechanism: when a pooled confusion matrix is used to correct subgroup-level conditional average treatment effect (CATE) estimates, the resulting bias is proportional to the product of misclassification heterogeneity ($\\Delta\\pi$) and CATE heterogeneity ($\\Delta\\tau$). In realistic settings, this ``debiasing'' worsens CATE bias by up to 7.8$\\times$ compared to no correction. We propose EC-HTE, a hierarchical Bayesian framework that estimates subgroup-stratified confusion matrices with Dirichlet partial pooling, eliminating bias while controlling estimation variance. EC-HTE requires as few as 250 expert labels (for $K \\le 4$ subgroups) and integrates with any CATE estimator including causal forests. On semi-synthetic political-science benchmarks with GPT-4.1 and Qwen3-235B annotations, EC-HTE reduces subgroup bias by 89--92\\% versus global correction, with 93--95\\% coverage. We further derive a diagnostic criterion (AUC = 0.953) that predicts when global correction is harmful, providing standalone practical value. Experiments on real interview data from Ashwin et al.\\ (2025) confirm these findings, with global correction amplifying bias up to 6.7$\\times$ on policy-relevant education subgroups.\n35\t\\end{abstract}\n36\t\n37\t\\section{Introduction}\n38\t\n39\tResearchers in computational social science increasingly rely on LLM annotations as effect modifiers in heterogeneous treatment effect analysis---for instance, classifying whether a political tweet contains ``economic framing'' to study how message framing moderates campaign effectiveness \\citep{egami2024dsl,angelopoulos2023ppi}. A growing body of work addresses LLM annotation error by applying correction methods that use a pooled confusion matrix estimated from expert-labeled calibration data \\citep{angelopoulos2023ppi,egami2024dsl,mozer2026arm}.\n40\t\n41\tHowever, LLM misclassification is not homogeneous. \\citet{ashwin2025refugee} document that GPT-4 annotation errors vary significantly by refugee status, gender, and education level. When such heterogeneous errors interact with heterogeneous treatment effects, the standard pooled correction---designed to remove bias---can instead \\emph{amplify} it.\n42\t\n43\tWe formalize this bias mechanism and show that applying a global confusion matrix correction to subgroup-level CATEs introduces bias proportional to $\\Delta\\pi \\times \\Delta\\tau$, the product of misclassification heterogeneity and CATE heterogeneity across subgroups. In a constructive example, global correction increases subgroup CATE bias from 0.023 to 0.179---a 7.8$\\times$ amplification. This finding has immediate practical consequence: researchers who responsibly apply measurement error correction may obtain \\emph{worse} estimates than those who use raw LLM labels.\n44\t\n45\tTo address this, we propose \\textbf{EC-HTE} (Error-Corrected Heterogeneous Treatment Effects), a hierarchical Bayesian framework that:\n46\t\\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n47\t\\item estimates \\emph{subgroup-stratified} confusion matrices using Dirichlet partial pooling, borrowing strength across subgroups while respecting heterogeneity;\n48\t\\item corrects CATE estimates at the subgroup level with properly propagated uncertainty;\n49\t\\item integrates with any CATE estimator, including causal forests."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_017CF8j9mavTxWyd4xGEWngg","session_id":"243f7ab5-0b31-4460-862b-4acef4243b5d","uuid":"ce99b9c1-a7d5-45cb-bd0f-05dd53545350","timestamp":"2026-05-20T20:18:20.192Z"}
