{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019PQUjxRCX1EBSwGZqiHoNe","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260520T185426\n3\ttier: worst\n4\toverall_score: 2.5/6 — Borderline Reject\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-20T19:38:05.807448+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Dream Review Report\n9\t\n10\t**Paper:** \"When Does Subgroup-Stratified Correction Matter? Measurement Error Propagation in Heterogeneous Treatment Effect Estimation with LLM Annotations\"\n11\t\n12\t**Review mode:** full | **Reviewers:** R1 (Methods & Experiments), R2 (Novelty & Related Work)\n13\t\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t## 1. Summary\n17\t\n18\tThe paper identifies a bias mechanism in HTE estimation when LLM annotations serve as effect modifiers: applying a pooled confusion matrix amplifies subgroup-level CATE bias in proportion to Δ_misclass × Δ_CATE. It proposes EC-HTE, a hierarchical Bayesian framework for subgroup-stratified confusion matrix estimation with partial pooling, integrated into honest causal forests. Through 11 experiments (synthetic, semi-synthetic, multi-LLM, real-data, robustness, budget sensitivity, PPCI comparison, allocation, and leaf-level integration), the paper reaches a largely negative conclusion: modern high-quality LLMs exhibit Δ_error ∈ [0.06, 0.09], well below the ~0.25 threshold where stratified correction is needed. EC-HTE provides only 8–18% improvement in realistic settings. A diagnostic product criterion (AUROC = 0.68) is offered as a screening heuristic.\n19\t\n20\t---\n21\t\n22\t## 2. Reviewer R1 — Methods & Experiments\n23\t\n24\t### Strengths\n25\t\n26\t**S1. Comprehensive experimental coverage.** All 11 planned experiments are executed and reported, spanning synthetic regime sweeps, semi-synthetic benchmarks with real LLM annotations (GPT-4.1, Qwen3-235B), real-data validation (fallback), PPCI baseline comparison, multi-LLM comparison, budget sensitivity, robustness analysis, adaptive allocation, and leaf-level integration. This is unusually thorough for a single paper.\n27\t\n28\t**S2. Honest reporting of unfavorable results.** The paper does not cherry-pick. It reports that EC-HTE is marginal in moderate settings (Table 1), that HB provides no significant advantage over MLE at n≥200 (Table 2, p≥0.18), that PPCI outperforms EC-HTE in realistic regimes (Table 12, ratio 1.07–1.18), and that the diagnostic criterion has weak AUROC (0.68). This scientific integrity is commendable.\n29\t\n30\t**S3. Bias bound verification is rigorous.** The product-structure bias formula (Eq. 1) is systematically verified on a parameter grid (R²=0.78), and the upper bound tightness is quantified (ratio ~0.55). The \"debiasing can hurt\" proposition is precisely bounded to 22% of parameter space at Δ_error > 0.25.\n31\t\n32\t**S4. Appropriate baselines.** Including PPCI (Sui et al. 2026) as a baseline is critical and the comparison is fair — the paper does not hide that PPCI is competitive or superior in realistic settings.\n33\t\n34\t### Weaknesses\n35\t\n36\t**W1. HB model description is insufficient.** Equations 3–5 specify the generative model but the paper provides no details on inference: which MCMC sampler (NUTS? Gibbs?), how many chains, iterations, warmup, convergence diagnostics (R-hat, ESS), or software used. This is a serious omission for a Bayesian methods paper. The leaf-level integration (Table 16) reports 15% convergence failures, but no diagnostics are shown.\n37\t\n38\t**W2. Coverage is systematically below 90% across all settings.** EC-HTE achieves 0.82–0.88 coverage for nominal 90% CIs (Tables 6, 7, 12, 14, 16). This is not a one-off issue but a systematic failure of uncertainty quantification. The paper acknowledges this in Limitations but provides no analysis of the cause (posterior under-dispersion? Dirichlet prior misspecification? Propagation of correction uncertainty into pseudo-outcomes?) or proposed fix. For a framework claiming to integrate correction into causal forest CIs, this is a core methodological shortcoming.\n39\t\n40\t**W3. Statistical significance absent for most comparisons.** Only Table 2 (HB vs MLE) provides p-values. The key comparisons — EC-HTE vs Global in semi-synthetic (12.5% improvement), EC-HTE vs PPCI (Table 12), EC-HTE vs Uncorrected in real data (Table 7, 10% improvement) — report only point estimates of bias with no confidence intervals, standard errors, or significance tests. It is impossible to assess whether reported differences are statistically meaningful.\n41\t\n42\t**W4. Primary real-data validation missing.** The Ashwin et al. (2025) refugee dataset was inaccessible due to ethical restrictions. The fallback to a single public CSS dataset with only 10% improvement provides weak validation. The paper effectively has no real-world causal inference application demonstrating practical value.\n43\t\n44\t**W5. Diagnostic criterion is not useful in practice.** AUROC=0.68 overall, dropping to 0.58 in the moderate regime (Table 9) — the very regime where guidance is most needed. At 90% sensitivity, FPR=0.45, meaning the criterion would unnecessarily recommend stratified correction in nearly half of cases. The NPV reframing (threshold < 0.02 → use global) is reasonable but limited: it only confidently rules OUT stratified correction, never rules it IN. A practitioner following this framework would almost always default to global correction anyway, making the framework's value marginal.\n45\t\n46\t**W6. PPCI dominates EC-HTE in all realistic settings.** Table 12 shows PPCI achieves lower or comparable bias in moderate (0.055 vs 0.065), homogeneous (0.028 vs 0.030), and semi-synthetic (0.032 vs 0.035) regimes, with consistently better coverage (0.87–0.89 vs 0.82–0.85) and 3× faster computation. EC-HTE only wins in the extreme regime (Δ_error=0.35), which the paper itself shows is empirically rare. The paper notes the two approaches are \"theoretically orthogonal\" and could be composed, but defers this entirely to future work. This is a critical gap.\n47\t\n48\t**W7. Expert budget requirements are prohibitive.** Crossover points of 500–1200 (Table 10) are far above the initially hypothesized 250 labels. Combined with the finding that the problem is small when it does occur, the cost-benefit of EC-HTE is unfavorable: large expert budgets for marginal improvement.\n49\t\n50\t**W8. Fragile robustness.** EC-HTE becomes worse than Global at ≥35% subgroup noise (Table 15). In real applications where subgroup definitions are derived from noisy NLP classifiers, 35% noise is not uncommon. The paper correctly identifies this but does not propose any mitigation.\n51\t\n52\t### R1 Questions for Authors\n53\t\n54\t1. What MCMC sampler was used? What were the convergence diagnostics (R-hat, ESS)? Can you provide trace plots or divergent transition counts?\n55\t2. Why is coverage systematically below 0.90? Have you investigated whether this is due to the Dirichlet prior, the pseudo-outcome construction, or the causal forest CI procedure?\n56\t3. Can you provide confidence intervals or bootstrap standard errors for the key bias comparisons (EC-HTE vs Global, EC-HTE vs PPCI)?\n57\t4. Given that PPCI outperforms EC-HTE in realistic settings, what is the argument for using EC-HTE in practice? Have you investigated combining EC-HTE + PPCI?\n58\t5. How sensitive are results to the Dirichlet hyperprior (Eq. 5)? Was any prior sensitivity analysis conducted?\n59\t\n60\t### R1 Preliminary Score: 3/6 (Weak Accept — as honest negative-result paper)\n61\t\n62\t---\n63\t\n64\t## 3. Reviewer R2 — Novelty & Related Work\n65\t\n66\t### Strengths\n67\t\n68\t**S1. Genuinely unexplored problem setting.** The specific scenario where the mismeasured variable *defines* the heterogeneity structure (effect modifier is the noisy covariate) is correctly identified as a gap. PPI, DSL, and Mozer all assume the effect modifier is well-measured or address ATE only. This problem identification is the paper's strongest intellectual contribution.\n69\t\n70\t**S2. Reframing as decision guidance is valuable.** Rather than claiming a universally superior method, the paper provides a decision framework (Section 4) that tells practitioners when to bother with stratified correction. This is practically useful even if the method itself is marginal.\n71\t\n72\t**S3. The negative finding is informative.** Demonstrating empirically that modern LLMs exhibit small Δ_error (0.06–0.09) and that global correction rarely hurts (amplification ≤1.4×) is useful knowledge for the CSS + causal inference community. The multi-LLM comparison (Table 13) showing r=-0.75 between LLM quality and Δ_error adds a forward-looking dimension.\n73\t\n74\t**S4. Abstract honesty is effective.** The abstract directly states the method's limitations and the narrowness of the problem. This is refreshing and builds credibility.\n75\t\n76\t### Weaknesses\n77\t\n78\t**W1. Theoretical contribution is shallow.** The bias formula (Eq. 1) — bias ∝ (C_g - C̄)(τ_g - τ̄) — is a first-order decomposition that follows directly from substituting a mismatched confusion matrix into the standard correction formula. This is not a deep theoretical insight; it is the expected result of applying a global estimator to a heterogeneous population. The upper bound (Eq. 2) uses the trivial infinity-norm bound. Neither result advances measurement error theory beyond what Carroll et al. (2006) would predict in this specific instantiation.\n79\t\n80\t**W2. HB model is a standard application, not a methodological contribution.** Hierarchical Dirichlet models for confusion matrices are well-established (e.g., Paun et al. 2018, Dawid & Skene 1979 in the annotator-modeling literature, Passonneau & Carpenter 2014). Applying this to subgroup-stratified misclassification in HTE is a reasonable engineering choice but does not constitute methodological novelty. The paper does not compare against other Bayesian annotator models or discuss this literature.\n81\t\n82\t**W3. The paper's three contributions are each individually weak.**\n83\t- Contribution 1 (bias characterization): Shallow theory, loose bound (0.55 tightness), product explains only 78% of variance.\n84\t- Contribution 2 (EC-HTE framework): Standard HB model that doesn't beat PPCI in realistic settings, has coverage problems, and requires large expert budgets.\n85\t- Contribution 3 (decision framework): AUROC 0.68, dropping to 0.58 in the critical moderate regime. The framework effectively says \"use global correction unless Δ_error > 0.25\" — which could be stated without the apparatus.\n86\t\n87\t**W4. Contribution framing is confused.** The paper tries simultaneously to be: (a) a theoretical analysis paper, (b) a methods paper, (c) a negative-result/decision-framework paper. None is executed deeply enough for a top venue. The theoretical depth is insufficient for (a), the method doesn't advance the state of the art for (b), and the negative result, while informative, lacks the deep analysis of mechanisms and boundary conditions expected for (c).\n88\t\n89\t**W5. Missing related work and thin bibliography.**\n90\t- Only 11 references for a paper spanning measurement error, causal inference, Bayesian methods, and LLM annotation — far too few.\n91\t- Missing: Bayesian annotator models (Dawid & Skene 1979, Passonneau & Carpenter 2014, Paun et al. 2018), which directly relate to hierarchical confusion matrix estimation.\n92\t- Missing: Sensitivity analysis frameworks in causal inference (e.g., Rosenbaum bounds, VanderWeele & Ding 2017 E-value) that address how measurement error affects causal conclusions.\n93\t- Missing: Recent work on calibration/reliability of LLM annotations beyond Gilardi et al. and Egami et al.\n94\t- Missing: Semi-parametric efficiency considerations — is EC-HTE efficient? What are the efficiency bounds?\n95\t\n96\t**W6. No figures in the paper.** The review context references Figures 1–17, but the actual paper contains zero figures. For a paper with 16 tables and complex experimental patterns (regime-dependent effects, crossover points, ROC curves, heatmaps), the absence of any visual communication is a major presentation failure. Key patterns (e.g., the narrow regime where EC-HTE helps, the crossover in robustness analysis, the ROC curve) would be far more compelling as figures than as table rows.\n97\t\n98\t**W7. Diminishing relevance threatens long-term impact.** The paper's own finding (Table 13, r=-0.75) suggests that as LLM quality improves, the problem EC-HTE addresses will largely disappear. This makes the work a snapshot of a transient phenomenon rather than a lasting contribution. The paper does not adequately address: under what future conditions might Δ_error increase? (E.g., harder tasks, lower-resource languages, domain shift.)\n99\t\n100\t**W8. Writing density is too high.** 16 tables in 9 pages of main text, many with similar structures. Tables 3 and 8 could be figures. Tables 10-11 could be combined. The experimental sections (3.1–3.11) read as a list of experimental results without sufficient narrative connective tissue explaining why each experiment follows from the previous one.\n101\t\n102\t### R2 Questions for Authors\n103\t\n104\t1. How does the bias formula (Eq. 1) differ from what classical measurement error theory (Carroll et al. 2006, Ch. 4) would predict for this specific estimand? What is genuinely new in the theoretical analysis?\n105\t2. Why was the Bayesian annotator modeling literature (Dawid & Skene, Paun et al.) not discussed? How does EC-HTE relate to these models?\n106\t3. Can you identify realistic scenarios where Δ_error > 0.25 would arise with modern LLMs? If not, what is the practical case for EC-HTE?\n107\t4. The paper has no figures. Was this a deliberate choice? Several results (ROC curve, robustness crossover, regime heatmap) would benefit enormously from visualization.\n108\t5. How would the analysis change for non-binary effect modifiers, multi-class outcomes, or continuous covariates discretized into subgroups?\n109\t\n110\t### R2 Preliminary Score: 2.5/6 (Borderline Reject)\n111\t\n112\t---\n113\t\n114\t## 4. Roundtable: Cross-Sharing & Convergence\n115\t\n116\t### Step 2: Cross-Sharing\n117\t\n118\t**R1 → R2:** The experimental results are comprehensive and honestly reported, but the lack of statistical significance testing (W3) and systematic coverage failure (W2) weaken the empirical foundations that R2 evaluates for impact. The PPCI comparison (W6) is the most damaging single result.\n119\t\n120\t**R2 → R1:** The novelty deficit (W1, W2) means the experiments need to deliver *very* strong results to compensate — but they don't. The theoretical shallowness means we can't interpret the experimental patterns (e.g., why does coverage fail?) through a principled lens.\n121\t\n122\t### Step 3: Convergence\n123\t\n124\tBoth reviewers agree on the following assessment:\n125\t\n126\t**Core tension:** The paper is commendably honest, but its own results systematically undermine all three contributions. The bias theory is shallow, the method doesn't beat PPCI in realistic settings, and the diagnostic criterion is too weak to be a standalone tool. The paper identifies a real gap (mismeasured effect modifiers in HTE) but shows that the gap doesn't matter much in practice.\n127\t\n128\t**The paper's value is primarily as a negative result** — \"don't over-engineer stratified correction for modern LLMs\" — but it doesn't commit fully to this framing. The decision framework (Section 4) is the strongest practical contribution, but it's underdeveloped (only half a page) relative to the extensive experimental apparatus that supports it.\n129\t\n130\t**Key deficiencies preventing acceptance:**\n131\t1. No clear methodological advance over existing alternatives (PPCI dominates in realistic settings)\n132\t2. Theoretical depth insufficient for an analysis paper\n133\t3. Systematic coverage failure without diagnosis or fix\n134\t4. No figures, thin bibliography, dense presentation\n135\t5. Missing statistical significance for key comparisons\n136\t6. The problem is shown to be small and shrinking — limited lasting impact\n137\t\n138\t**Potential path to acceptance:** Commit fully to the \"systematic analysis + decision framework\" narrative. Drop the claim that EC-HTE is a contribution; instead, treat it as one tool in the comparison. Deepen the theoretical analysis (tighter bounds, efficiency considerations). Add figures. Discuss when Δ_error could be large (harder NLP tasks, multilingual settings, domain shift). Compare against Bayesian annotator models. Provide the EC-HTE + PPCI composition experiment.\n139\t\n140\t---\n141\t\n142\t## 5. Consolidated Assessment\n143\t\n144\t### Overall Score: 2.5 / 6 (Borderline Reject)\n145\t\n146\t| Dimension | Score | Notes |\n147\t|-----------|-------|-------|\n148\t| Soundness | 3.0 | Experiments are thorough but lack significance tests; coverage failure undiagnosed |\n149\t| Novelty | 2.0 | Bias formula is straightforward; HB model is standard; negative result is informative but thin |\n150\t| Significance | 2.5 | Problem shown to be small for modern LLMs; diminishing relevance; PPCI dominates in practice |\n151\t| Clarity | 3.0 | Writing is clear but too dense; zero figures; 16 tables in 9 pages |\n152\t| Overall | 2.5 | Honest paper on a real problem that turns out to be minor in practice. Contributions individually weak. |\n153\t\n154\t### Verdict\n155\t\n156\tThe paper identifies a genuine gap — measurement error propagation when the mismeasured variable defines HTE structure — but its own extensive experiments demonstrate that this gap is narrow and the proposed method does not clearly advance the state of the art. The theoretical contribution is a first-order decomposition without deep insight; the HB framework is a standard model that underperforms PPCI in realistic settings with worse coverage and higher computational cost; and the diagnostic criterion has weak discriminative power. The paper is honest and thorough, which is commendable, but honesty about weak results does not by itself constitute a publishable contribution at a top ML venue.\n157\t\n158\t**Recommendation:** Reject with encouragement to resubmit after (1) committing to the negative-result / decision-framework framing as the core contribution, (2) deepening theoretical analysis, (3) adding the EC-HTE + PPCI composition experiment, (4) addressing coverage failure, and (5) adding figures and broadening the bibliography.\n159\t\n160\t### Key Questions for Authors (Consolidated)\n161\t\n162\t1. What MCMC sampler, convergence diagnostics, and software were used for the HB model?\n163\t2. What causes the systematic coverage underperformance (0.82–0.88 vs 0.90 target)?\n164\t3. Can you provide confidence intervals for key method comparisons?\n165\t4. How does EC-HTE compose with PPCI? This seems like the natural next step.\n166\t5. How does the bias formula differ from what Carroll et al. (2006) would predict?\n167\t6. Under what realistic conditions might Δ_error exceed 0.25? (Harder tasks, multilingual, domain shift?)\n168\t7. Why is the Bayesian annotator modeling literature not discussed?\n169\t\n170\t---\n171\t\n172\t## 6. Experiment Coverage Check\n173\t\n174\t| Planned Experiment | In Paper? | Section | Quality |\n175\t|--------------------|-----------|---------|---------|\n176\t| exp-001: HB synthetic validation | Yes | §3.1 | Tables 1-2, adequate |\n177\t| exp-002: Bias bound verification | Yes | §3.2 | Tables 3-4, adequate |\n178\t| exp-003: Semi-synthetic benchmark | Yes | §3.3 | Tables 5-6, adequate |\n179\t| exp-004: Ashwin real-data | Partial | §3.4 | Primary data inaccessible; fallback only (Table 7) |\n180\t| exp-005: Diagnostic criterion | Yes | §3.5 | Tables 8-9, adequate |\n181\t| exp-006: Expert budget sensitivity | Yes | §3.6 | Tables 10-11, adequate |\n182\t| exp-007: PPCI comparison | Yes | §3.7 | Table 12, adequate |\n183\t| exp-008: Multi-LLM comparison | Yes | §3.8 | Table 13, adequate |\n184\t| exp-009: Adaptive allocation | Yes | §3.9 | Table 14, adequate |\n185\t| exp-010: Robustness | Yes | §3.10 | Table 15, adequate |\n186\t| exp-011: Leaf-level integration | Yes | §3.11 | Table 16, adequate |\n187\t\n188\tCoverage: 10/11 fully executed, 1/11 partial (data access issue). No planned experiments were omitted without explanation. However, **zero figures** were included despite the review context describing 17 planned figures — this is a major presentation gap."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01FX1n1RMdseirwuNuQ9WE48","session_id":"243f7ab5-0b31-4460-862b-4acef4243b5d","uuid":"3b5ac120-4ac9-4a32-b50a-3edd7fc51971","timestamp":"2026-05-20T20:18:50.276Z"}
