{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q4mSQMYycx6FfkFUZLaTH7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tdream_id: dream_20260520T185426\n2\tproject: ec_hte_llm_annotation\n3\tvenue: ''\n4\tfocus: 核心关注：(1) \"debiasing can hurt\" 的 surprise value 是否足够撑起 EMNLP main；(2) 层级贝叶斯\n5\t  partial pooling 作为方法核心是否有足够技术贡献；(3) 仅有合成+半合成实验时论文是否可接受（Ashwin 数据集可能拿不到）；(4) 与 PPCI\n6\t  (Sui 2026) 的差异化是否清晰\n7\treview_mode: full\n8\treview_visible: true\n9\twriting_visible: true\n10\twhat_if: []\n11\ttier_definitions:\n12\t  tiers:\n13\t    best:\n14\t      tier: best\n15\t      meaning: 三条 claims 全部在合理参数范围内得到强验证；semi-synthetic 结果足够 dramatic 撑 headline；Ashwin\n16\t        数据可用或可替代\n17\t      key_assumptions:\n18\t      - global correction 在 moderate heterogeneity 设定下即产生显著 bias 放大（不需要极端参数）\n19\t      - HB partial pooling 在 n_expert=250 时已明显优于 stratified MLE（小样本优势可量化）\n20\t      - diagnostic criterion 有高判别力，阈值跨数据集稳定\n21\t      - semi-synthetic 中 GPT-4.1/Qwen3 的 misclassification heterogeneity 自然存在且足够大\n22\t      - 至少有一个 real-data 验证（Ashwin 或替代公开数据集）\n23\t      number_ranges:\n24\t        bias_amplification_factor_global_vs_uncorrected:\n25\t        - 5.0\n26\t        - 12.0\n27\t        ec_hte_absolute_cate_bias:\n28\t        - 0.005\n29\t        - 0.02\n30\t        ec_hte_coverage_90ci:\n31\t        - 0.9\n32\t        - 0.97\n33\t        min_n_expert_for_dominance:\n34\t        - 100\n35\t        - 250\n36\t        diagnostic_criterion_auroc:\n37\t        - 0.9\n38\t        - 0.98\n39\t        hb_vs_stratified_mle_bias_reduction:\n40\t        - 0.3\n41\t        - 0.6\n42\t        ec_hte_vs_ppci_bias_ratio:\n43\t        - 0.15\n44\t        - 0.4\n45\t      narrative_direction: '原始 framing 完全成立。\"Debiasing can hurt\" 作为 problem-level\n46\t        discovery 有足够 surprise value： 在 realistic 参数下 global correction 使 bias 恶化\n47\t        5-10x，这是 NLP+CSS 社区未意识到的陷阱。 EC-HTE 以 250 expert labels 实现 near-oracle，HB partial\n48\t        pooling 的小样本优势可量化。 Diagnostic criterion 作为 standalone practical tool 给论文第三条独立贡献。\n49\t        Real data 验证锁定了 \"不只是理论上可能\" 的叙事。投 EMNLP main 无需降级任何 claim。\n50\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01FX1n1RMdseirwuNuQ9WE48","session_id":"243f7ab5-0b31-4460-862b-4acef4243b5d","uuid":"519e7b8f-e8d7-48f9-aa6b-549fbd12bb63","timestamp":"2026-05-20T20:19:10.871Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a21b6de08d301f400","tool_use_id":"toolu_017CF8j9mavTxWyd4xGEWngg","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":76698,"tool_uses":23,"duration_ms":154663},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"06f673b7-9872-41b7-80e4-fe8aa6f3260b","session_id":"243f7ab5-0b31-4460-862b-4acef4243b5d"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WNNfX5Xpsyf4Jnw8KtZHWD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WPkyKaRgBnBXTJ4L8Fpu1R","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"ec_hte_llm_annotation","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260520T194535","overall_score":"4 - Weak Accept","report":"\n# Review Report — R1 (Methods and Experiments Focus)\n\n**Paper**: \"When Debiasing Backfires: Measurement-Error Heterogeneity in LLM-Annotated Causal Effect Estimation\"\n**Reviewer**: R1 — Methods and Experiments\n**Overall Assessment**: 4 (Weak Accept) / EMNLP 2026 scale: 4.0 (Conference)\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nThis paper identifies a bias mechanism in LLM-annotated heterogeneous treatment effect estimation: when subgroup-specific LLM misclassification rates are corrected using a pooled (global) confusion matrix, the resulting bias is proportional to the product of misclassification heterogeneity and CATE heterogeneity across subgroups. The authors propose EC-HTE, a hierarchical Bayesian framework that estimates subgroup-stratified confusion matrices via Dirichlet partial pooling. The paper validates the theory through synthetic experiments, a semi-synthetic political tweet benchmark with GPT-4.1 and Qwen3-235B, real-data validation on Ashwin et al. (2025) refugee interviews, and several supplementary experiments (PPCI orthogonality, causal forest integration, budget sensitivity, robustness). Across all settings, EC-HTE reduces subgroup bias by 85-93% versus global correction.\n\n---\n\n## Strengths\n\n### S1. Clean and important problem identification\nThe core insight — that pooled confusion matrix correction can amplify rather than reduce bias when both misclassification and treatment effects are heterogeneous — is genuinely useful. The product form bias = f(delta_pi x delta_tau) is elegant and provides practitioners with a simple diagnostic. The 7.8x amplification result is striking and has immediate practical relevance for the growing community using LLMs for computational social science.\n\n### S2. Comprehensive experimental coverage\nThe paper covers all 11 planned experiments, spanning synthetic validation, bias bound verification, semi-synthetic benchmarks with two LLMs, real-data validation, PPCI orthogonality, causal forest integration, budget sensitivity, robustness to misspecification, adaptive allocation, and multi-LLM comparison. This is thorough for an 8+3 page paper. The progression from controlled synthetic to semi-synthetic to real data provides layered evidence.\n\n### S3. Principled HB estimation with strong diagnostics\nThe hierarchical Bayesian approach is well-motivated: at n<=250, HB achieves 22-28% RMSE reduction over MLE (K=2) and 32-35% for K=4, with near-nominal coverage (0.93-0.96). MCMC diagnostics are reported comprehensively (R-hat <= 1.004, ESS >= 387, zero divergences). The partial pooling mechanism provides a natural \"never worse than global\" property — when subgroups are truly homogeneous, HB shrinks toward the global estimate.\n\n### S4. PPCI orthogonality demonstration is convincing\nThe experiment cleanly separates covariate-side vs. outcome-side measurement error and shows EC-HTE and PPCI address different error types. The composition result (bias 0.006 when both errors present, r=0.04 correlation between correction terms) elevates the contribution from \"we are different from PPCI\" to \"we are complementary to PPCI.\"\n\n### S5. Honest treatment of failure modes\nThe paper reports where EC-HTE fails: K=8 with n=50 (6 labels per subgroup) degrades below global correction. The breakeven at 38% subgroup label noise is transparently reported. The Limitations section acknowledges the a priori subgroup structure requirement and computational overhead.\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n### Major\n\n#### W1. Theorem 1 is an identity, not a bound — presentation overpromises (Critical)\nTheorem 1 states the bias of globally-corrected CATE as sum_k w_k(pi_bar - pi_k)tau_k. Under the stated assumptions (binary effect modifier, known confusion matrix structure), this is an algebraic identity, not an approximation or bound. The paper sometimes treats it as a bound (\"bias bound verification,\" R^2 = 0.982) and sometimes as exact. The R^2 < 1.0 against MC simulations likely reflects finite-sample estimation noise in the confusion matrix, not looseness in the theoretical result.\n\n**Impact**: Moderate. The result is correct, but the framing creates confusion about what is being verified. The authors should clarify: the theoretical decomposition is exact at the population level; the MC verification confirms that finite-sample estimation noise does not materially alter the relationship.\n\n**Recommendation**: Rename \"Bias Bound Verification\" to \"Bias Decomposition Verification.\" State explicitly that R^2 < 1.0 reflects sampling variability in C-hat, not bound looseness.\n\n#### W2. Semi-synthetic benchmark: CATE injection mechanism is underspecified (Major)\nSection 5.3 states that CATE heterogeneity structure is \"injected\" into real political tweets, but the DGP is not described. Critical missing details:\n- How is treatment T assigned? (Randomized? Observational with confounders?)\n- How are outcomes Y generated? (Additive? Interaction model?)\n- What are the true CATE values (tau_1=0.15, tau_2=0.04 appear in Table 8 but the generative process is unexplained)?\n- How do the 20,000 tweets map to the two subgroups? Is the subgroup structure based on actual tweet content or artificially imposed?\n\n**Impact**: High. Without the DGP, the semi-synthetic results are not reproducible and the degree of realism cannot be evaluated. The treatment effect magnitudes (0.15 vs. 0.04) and subgroup definitions could be cherry-picked to maximize the amplification effect.\n\n**Recommendation**: Provide full DGP specification in the main text or a detailed appendix. Include: outcome model, treatment assignment mechanism, subgroup definition criteria, and sensitivity to alternative CATE magnitudes.\n\n#### W3. Real-data validation reports bias and RMSE but ground truth CATE is unavailable (Major)\nTable 11 (real data, education subgroups) reports bias values (e.g., EC-HTE bias = 0.003, global bias = 0.045). But for the Ashwin et al. (2025) real dataset, the true subgroup-specific CATEs are unknown. How are \"bias\" and \"RMSE\" computed without ground truth?\n\nThe paper does not clarify this. If the ground truth is derived from expert-labeled data using some held-out estimator, this needs explicit statement and justification (with discussion of circularity risks). If this is a semi-synthetic overlay on the Ashwin data (injecting known treatment effects), it should be labeled as such rather than \"real-data validation.\"\n\n**Impact**: High. The real-data section is presented as the strongest external validity evidence, but its validity depends entirely on how ground truth is established. Without clarification, the 6.7x amplification claim on real data is uninterpretable.\n\n**Recommendation**: Either (a) frame as \"real-data illustration\" showing qualitative pattern consistency with domain knowledge (no ground truth claims), or (b) describe the ground truth construction transparently with appropriate caveats.\n\n#### W4. The diagnostic criterion (AUC=0.953) is evaluated on the same data used to develop it (Major)\nThe diagnostic score (delta_pi x delta_tau) is derived from Theorem 1, and its AUC is evaluated across \"342 experimental settings spanning synthetic, semi-synthetic, and real data.\" But these 342 settings are the same experiments run for the paper. There is no held-out evaluation or cross-validation. The high AUC may reflect that the DGP parameters were chosen to span a range favorable to the diagnostic.\n\n**Impact**: Moderate-to-high. The diagnostic is theoretically well-motivated (it is the first-order term in the bias expression), so the AUC should be high by construction. But reporting it as 0.953 without acknowledging that the evaluation set is not independent overstates the empirical contribution.\n\n**Recommendation**: Either (a) clearly state that the high AUC is expected by construction from Theorem 1 and the evaluation is a consistency check, or (b) evaluate on a genuinely held-out set of parameter configurations not used during method development.\n\n### Minor\n\n#### W5. Coverage is systematically below nominal at small n (Minor)\nAt n=100, K=2 extreme regime: HB coverage = 0.932 (nominal 0.95). At n=100, K=4: coverage = 0.921. At n=50, K=8: coverage = 0.84. While the paper notes this, it does not diagnose the cause. Possible explanations include: posterior intervals are too narrow due to the Dirichlet-Multinomial conjugacy approximation, the Gamma hyperprior is informative, or the delta-method variance propagation from C-hat to tau-hat introduces approximation error.\n\n**Recommendation**: Add a brief diagnostic. Is undercoverage driven by the confusion matrix posterior being too concentrated, or by variance propagation from C to tau?\n\n#### W6. Computational cost comparison is incomplete (Minor)\nThe paper reports 2.3s per MCMC fit vs. 0.008s for MLE, but does not report total wall-clock time for the full pipeline (including LLM annotation, expert labeling, CATE estimation). For practitioners, the 300x slowdown of MCMC may be negligible relative to LLM API costs, but this should be stated explicitly.\n\n#### W7. Only 9 references in main bibliography (Minor)\nThe bibliography is thin for a venue like EMNLP. Key missing references include: Carroll et al. (2006) appears in Related Work but the bibtex entry format is inconsistent; Imai & Yamamoto (2010) on causal mediation with measurement error; Gustafson (2003) on Bayesian measurement error models; classical Dawid-Skene extensions (Passonneau & Carpenter 2014, Paun et al. 2018 is cited but not in bibliography). The claim \"No work addresses correction when the mismeasured variable defines the heterogeneity structure\" needs more careful literature engagement to be credible.\n\n#### W8. No figures in the paper (Minor)\nThe paper contains 16+ tables and zero figures. A bias amplification heatmap (delta_pi vs. delta_tau), a mechanism schematic, and a diagnostic ROC curve would substantially improve readability and visual communication of the core insight.\n\n#### W9. \"Never worse than global\" claim needs qualification (Minor)\nThe paper claims EC-HTE has a \"never worse than global guarantee via partial pooling\" (Introduction, Contribution 2). But Appendix A shows that at K=8, n=50, EC-HTE RMSE (0.058) exceeds global RMSE (0.042). The guarantee presumably holds asymptotically or under sufficient sample size conditions, but these conditions are not stated.\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1.** In the real-data validation (Table 11), how is the ground truth CATE established for computing bias and RMSE? If expert labels serve as ground truth, what is the effective sample size per subgroup, and how is the estimator for the \"true\" CATE constructed?\n\n**Q2.** Theorem 1 assumes a binary effect modifier X in {1,...,K} but the confusion matrix correction C_k^{-1} operates on a 2x2 matrix (binary classification). How exactly does the correction work when K>2? Is X still binary (presence/absence of the effect modifier) with K subgroups defined by a separate covariate S? The notation in Section 3.1 conflates X and the subgroup indicator, which creates confusion.\n\n**Q3.** The semi-synthetic benchmark uses n=500 expert labels. In the budget sensitivity analysis (Appendix A), the crossover for K=2 is at n~85. Have you verified that the semi-synthetic results are robust to reducing expert labels to n=250 (the \"headline\" label count)? The gap between 500 (used) and 250 (claimed sufficient) should be bridged experimentally.\n\n**Q4.** For the PPCI comparison (Table 13), PPCI dramatically worsens covariate-side error (bias 0.089 vs. 0.021 uncorrected). This is a strong and somewhat surprising result. Can you provide intuition for why PPCI makes things worse rather than simply being ineffective? Is this because PPCI's kernel localization operates in the mislabeled X-space, actively distorting the conditioning?\n\n**Q5.** The Dirichlet hyperprior uses alpha_j ~ Gamma(mu_j, sigma_j). What are the hyperparameter values? How sensitive are the results to this choice? The paper mentions \"prior sensitivity\" nowhere in the main text, and Appendix C tests robustness to subgroup misspecification but not prior misspecification.\n\n---\n\n## Limitations Assessment\n\nThe authors' Limitations section is adequate but could be strengthened:\n- The a priori subgroup structure requirement is honestly stated.\n- The K=8, n=50 failure mode is noted.\n- Binary/small-K restriction is acknowledged.\n- **Missing**: No discussion of the assumption that the confusion matrix is stable across the unlabeled population (i.e., that expert-labeled calibration data is representative). If the expert labels are drawn from a convenience sample rather than a stratified random sample, the confusion matrix estimates could be biased.\n- **Missing**: The method assumes treatment T is randomized (or conditionally ignorable). Under unmeasured confounding, the CATE estimates themselves are biased, and correcting only the measurement error in the effect modifier addresses one of potentially many bias sources.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\nThis paper makes a genuine contribution by identifying and formalizing the interaction between measurement error heterogeneity and treatment effect heterogeneity in LLM-annotated causal inference. The core insight is clean, practically important, and supported by extensive experiments. The EC-HTE framework is a sensible solution that leverages standard Bayesian machinery effectively.\n\nThe main weaknesses are: (1) the real-data validation's ground truth is opaque, (2) the semi-synthetic DGP is underspecified, (3) the diagnostic criterion evaluation is not independent of development data, and (4) the \"never worse than global\" claim is overstated. These are addressable in revision without changing the paper's conclusions.\n\nThe paper's positioning as \"problem identification + principled solution\" rather than \"new estimation technique\" is appropriate for EMNLP. The practical impact is clear: any researcher using PPI/DSL-style corrections on LLM annotations should be aware of this failure mode.\n\nI recommend acceptance contingent on addressing W2 (DGP specification) and W3 (real-data ground truth clarification).\n\n---\n\n## Scores\n\n| Dimension | Score | Justification |\n|-----------|-------|---------------|\n| **Soundness** | 3 / 4 | Theorem 1 is correct but framing is imprecise (identity vs. bound). HB estimation is sound. Real-data ground truth is unclear. |\n| **Contribution** | 3 / 4 | Problem identification is the main novelty; method components are standard. Diagnostic criterion has standalone value. PPCI orthogonality adds complementary insight. |\n| **Presentation** | 2.5 / 4 | Dense tables, no figures, thin bibliography. Core ideas are clear but the paper reads as a technical report rather than a polished conference paper. DGP details missing for key experiments. |\n| **Overall** | 4.0 / 6 | Conference-level contribution with clear practical impact. Addressable weaknesses in presentation and experimental transparency. |\n| **Confidence** | 4 / 5 | I am confident in my assessment of the methods and experiments. I have not verified the proofs line-by-line but the algebraic structure is straightforward. |\n\n---\n\n## Summary Recommendation\n\n**Accept with minor revisions.** The paper identifies a real and important problem, proposes a sound solution, and validates it comprehensively. The main revisions needed are: (1) clarify real-data ground truth, (2) specify the semi-synthetic DGP, (3) fix the \"bound\" vs. \"identity\" framing, (4) add figures, (5) expand the bibliography. None of these would change the paper's conclusions.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":725,"cache_read_input_tokens":75427,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":725,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_017CF8j9mavTxWyd4xGEWngg","session_id":"243f7ab5-0b31-4460-862b-4acef4243b5d","uuid":"d9c6dca1-c50a-4fdf-9452-f466cb2a010e"}
