{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TEAnEYcZxS58b1odtpUh62","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260520T194535\n3\ttier: best\n4\toverall_score: 4/6 Weak Accept\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-20T20:21:49.054693+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Dream Review Report: \"When Debiasing Backfires: Measurement-Error Heterogeneity in LLM-Annotated Causal Effect Estimation\"\n9\t\n10\t**Review Mode**: Full (2 Reviewers, 4-step roundtable)\n11\t**Date**: 2026-05-20 (Round 2)\n12\t**Focus**: Novelty, Related Work, and Positioning (R2)\n13\t\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t## Overall Assessment\n17\t\n18\t**Score: 4 / 6 (Weak Accept)**\n19\t\n20\tThis paper identifies a genuine and well-formalized failure mode at the intersection of LLM annotation and heterogeneous treatment effect estimation. The core insight -- that global confusion matrix correction amplifies subgroup CATE bias in proportion to Delta-pi x Delta-tau -- is novel in its specific formalization and practically consequential. Compared to Round 1, the paper has materially improved: MCMC implementation details are now present (PyMC/NUTS, R-hat <= 1.004, ESS >= 387), the K>2 sum-of-products extension is explicitly stated and verified (Remark 1, Table 3), real-data validation on Ashwin et al. (2025) is included (Table 8-9), and secondary experiments have been moved to appendix, freeing space for deeper core discussion. However, the bibliography remains critically thin (9 references), novelty positioning still needs sharpening, and several related work gaps persist. These are fixable within one revision cycle.\n21\t\n22\t---\n23\t\n24\t## Summary\n25\t\n26\tThe paper formalizes a bias mechanism in HTE estimation with LLM-annotated effect modifiers: applying a pooled confusion matrix to correct subgroup-level CATEs introduces bias proportional to the product of misclassification heterogeneity and CATE heterogeneity. The proposed EC-HTE framework uses hierarchical Bayesian Dirichlet partial pooling to estimate subgroup-stratified confusion matrices, correcting CATEs at the subgroup level. Experiments span synthetic parameter sweeps (R^2 = 0.982), semi-synthetic political tweet benchmarks with GPT-4.1 and Qwen3-235B (89-92% bias reduction), real refugee interview data from Ashwin et al. (6.7x amplification corrected), PPCI orthogonality demonstration, and causal forest integration. A diagnostic criterion (AUC = 0.953) predicts when global correction is harmful.\n27\t\n28\t---\n29\t\n30\t## Strengths\n31\t\n32\t**S1. Novel and well-scoped problem identification.** The specific failure mode -- that error correction *amplifies* bias when the mismeasured variable defines the heterogeneity structure -- has not been formalized before. The Delta-pi x Delta-tau product characterization (Theorem 1) is clean, intuitive, and verifiable. This is the paper's strongest contribution. While the underlying algebra is not deep, the *recognition* that this matters in the increasingly common LLM-annotation-for-CSS pipeline is genuinely new. Neither PPI (Angelopoulos et al. 2023), DSL (Egami et al. 2024), Mozer (2026), nor PPCI (Sui et al. 2026) address this case.\n33\t\n34\t**S2. Well-executed PPCI orthogonality demonstration (Section 5.5).** This is the paper's second-strongest contribution from a positioning standpoint. Rather than merely claiming EC-HTE differs from PPCI, the paper demonstrates empirically that (a) each method excels exclusively on its target error type, (b) PPCI actually *worsens* covariate-side error (bias 0.089 vs 0.021 uncorrected), and (c) their composition achieves the best result (bias 0.006). The near-zero correction-term correlation (r = 0.04) is convincing. This transforms a defensive distinction into a constructive contribution.\n35\t\n36\t**S3. Real-data validation now included.** The Ashwin et al. (2025) refugee interview results (Table 8-9) close the most critical gap from Round 1. The 6.7x amplification on education subgroups, producing a misleading *negative* CATE estimate for the low-education group, is compelling and policy-relevant. The cross-dimension consistency (4.2-6.7x amplification correlating with error ratio) is persuasive.\n37\t\n38\t**S4. MCMC implementation details now present.** Table 2 provides R-hat, ESS, divergence counts, and runtime. PyMC with NUTS, 4 chains, 1000 warmup. All diagnostics pass standard thresholds. This resolves the Round 1 reproducibility gap.\n39\t\n40\t**S5. Theory-experiment alignment improved.** Remark 1 explicitly extends the bias formula to general K via the sum-of-products form, with Table 3 providing K=4 verification (R^2 = 0.971). The theory-experiment gap from Round 1 is substantially narrowed.\n41\t\n42\t**S6. Honest and calibrated claims.** The paper correctly positions EC-HTE as \"problem identification + principled solution\" rather than a new estimation technique. Limitations are forthright about K=8 failure with 50 labels, computational overhead, and the a priori subgroup requirement. The \"never worse than global\" framing via partial pooling is stated as a practical property, not a theorem.\n43\t\n44\t**S7. Diagnostic criterion has genuine standalone value.** AUC = 0.953 with a stable threshold (0.008) across synthetic/semi-synthetic/real data gives practitioners a concrete decision rule from pilot annotations. Even researchers who do not adopt EC-HTE can use this diagnostic to assess whether their global correction is trustworthy.\n45\t\n46\t---\n47\t\n48\t## Weaknesses\n49\t\n50\t### Major\n51\t\n52\t**W1. [Major] Bibliography remains critically thin (9 references).** This was the #1 structural issue flagged unanimously in Round 1, and it has not been addressed. For a paper bridging measurement error theory, Bayesian estimation, causal inference, LLM annotation, and computational social science, 9 references is far below the 30-40+ expected at EMNLP. Critical omissions include:\n53\t\n54\t- **Classical measurement error**: Gustafson (2003, *Measurement Error and Misclassification in Statistics and Epidemiology*); Hu & Schennach (2008) on semiparametric identification under misclassification; Battistin & Chesher (2014) on bunching with misclassification; Lewbel (2007) on endogenous regressor corrections. The paper cites only Carroll et al. (2006) -- a textbook -- without engaging the extensive subsequent literature on when misclassification correction can amplify MSE (a known phenomenon in the statistics literature since at least Fuller 1987).\n55\t- **Bayesian annotator models**: Only Dawid & Skene (1979) and Paun et al. (2018) are cited. Missing: Passonneau & Carpenter (2014), Hovy et al. (2013), Rodrigues & Pereira (2018, learning from crowds with deep learning), Kim & Ghahramani (2012). The connection between EC-HTE's HB Dirichlet model and this literature is underexplored.\n56\t- **LLM-as-annotator in CSS**: Tornberg (2024), Pangakis et al. (2023), Gilardi et al. (2023), Ziems et al. (2024) -- none cited. These papers document the heterogeneous error patterns that motivate EC-HTE.\n57\t- **HTE estimation literature**: Wager & Athey (2018) for honest causal forests (used but not cited!), Kennedy (2023) for DR-learner, Kunzel et al. (2019) for X-learner, Nie & Wager (2021) for R-learner, Chernozhukov et al. (2018) for DML. The paper uses CausalForestDML without citing the foundational work.\n58\t- **PPI extensions**: Angelopoulos et al. (2023b, PPI++), Zrnic & Candes (2024, active statistical inference). The broader PPI ecosystem is relevant.\n59\t- **Multiple imputation for misclassified variables**: Blackwell et al. (2017), Cole et al. (2006) -- natural baselines that are neither cited nor compared against.\n60\t\n61\tThis omission is no longer merely a presentation issue; it actively undermines the novelty claim. A reader familiar with the measurement error literature may reasonably ask whether the \"debiasing can backfire\" insight was already known (and it partially was -- Fuller 1987 discusses variance inflation under misclassification correction). The paper needs to engage with this literature to establish what specifically is new here.\n62\t\n63\t**W2. [Major] Novelty positioning needs further sharpening against classical measurement error theory.** The core theoretical result -- that applying a global correction to heterogeneous subgroups introduces bias proportional to the deviation from the global error rate times the subgroup effect -- is a specific instantiation of a well-known phenomenon in the measurement error literature. Carroll et al. (2006, Ch. 4) discuss how misspecifying the measurement error distribution leads to biased corrections. The Delta-pi x Delta-tau product form is new in this specific CATE context, but the *qualitative insight* that applying the wrong correction can worsen estimates is not.\n64\t\n65\tThe paper should explicitly engage with this: \"It has long been known in the measurement error literature that misspecified corrections can amplify MSE (Fuller 1987, Sec. 2.3). Our contribution is (a) the specific quantitative characterization for CATE estimation when the mismeasured variable defines the heterogeneity structure, (b) demonstrating that this occurs by default in the LLM-annotation workflow where global confusion matrices are standard practice, and (c) providing a computable diagnostic and correction framework.\" Without this contextualization, a statistics-trained reviewer may perceive the theoretical contribution as trivially derivable.\n66\t\n67\t**W3. [Major] The Ashwin et al. (2025) reference appears problematic.** The bibliography entry reads \"Ashwin, Maaz et al. 2025. Differential LLM annotation errors across demographic subgroups in refugee interview data. Working paper.\" But in the paper_framing.md the authors are listed as \"Ashwin, Aditya; Swanson, Becca; Blair, Robert; Samii, Cyrus\" and the year is 2025 with a different title (\"Using Large Language Models for Qualitative Analysis can Introduce Serious Bias\"). The main text says \"Ashwin et al. (2025)\" for the refugee data. The bibliography entry has incorrect first author name (\"Maaz\" vs \"Aditya\") and a different paper title than what the framing document describes. This needs to be verified and corrected. If this is a working paper without a stable URL, its verifiability as ground-truth evidence is weakened.\n68\t\n69\t### Moderate\n70\t\n71\t**W4. [Moderate] Missing natural baselines.** SIMEX (Simulation-Extrapolation, Cook & Stefanski 1994) and multiple imputation for misclassified categorical variables (Blackwell et al. 2017) are standard measurement error correction baselines absent from the experiments. The paper compares EC-HTE against no correction, global correction, MLE stratified, and PPCI, but omits the most obvious classical alternatives. Even if these methods are expected to perform poorly (they likely would, because they don't stratify by subgroup), their absence leaves a gap in the experimental narrative.\n72\t\n73\t**W5. [Moderate] DSL distinction remains insufficiently crisp.** Egami et al. (2024) DSL is a direct competitor for the CSS+LLM audience. Both EC-HTE and DSL use confusion matrix estimation. The key distinction -- DSL applies a global correction appropriate for ATE because it targets population-level estimands, while EC-HTE needs subgroup-specific corrections because the mismeasured variable defines the conditioning structure for HTE -- should be stated in a single, prominent sentence. Currently the reader must infer this from the Related Work paragraph.\n74\t\n75\t**W6. [Moderate] Invertibility assumption unstated.** Equation (3) applies C_k^{-1}. For binary X, this requires det(C_k) != 0, i.e., the classifier must beat random chance per subgroup. For small K and small n_k, some subgroup confusion matrices may be near-singular, especially under the HB prior at small n. This assumption should be stated, and the HB prior's role in preventing near-singularity should be discussed.\n76\t\n77\t**W7. [Moderate] \"Never worse than global\" claim is informal.** The paper states this as a property of partial pooling but never formally proves it. In the extreme case (K=8, n=50), EC-HTE is worse than global (Appendix A, Table A.3). The claim should be qualified: \"Under reasonable per-subgroup sample sizes (n_k >= 30), EC-HTE's partial pooling guarantees non-inferior performance to global correction.\" As stated, it is contradicted by the paper's own robustness results.\n78\t\n79\t### Minor\n80\t\n81\t**W8. [Minor] Semi-synthetic ground truth design.** The semi-synthetic setup injects a known CATE structure into real tweet data. The resulting \"ground truth\" CATEs are by construction perfectly aligned with the binary subgroup structure. In practice, subgroup boundaries are fuzzy, and CATE heterogeneity may not partition cleanly. The robustness analysis (Appendix C) partially addresses this, but the main semi-synthetic results may overstate EC-HTE's advantage relative to noisier real-world settings.\n82\t\n83\t**W9. [Minor] No figures in main text.** While tables are informative, key patterns -- the bias amplification heatmap (Delta-pi vs Delta-tau), the diagnostic ROC curve, the bias mechanism schematic -- would be far more effective as figures. The paper's 13 tables in 8 pages of main text (plus 4 appendix tables) make it read more as a technical report than a conference paper. At minimum, a mechanism schematic and the amplification heatmap should be in the main text.\n84\t\n85\t---\n86\t\n87\t## Questions for Authors\n88\t\n89\t1. **Classical measurement error context**: How does Theorem 1 relate to the well-known result that misspecified measurement error corrections can increase MSE (Fuller 1987, Sec. 2.3; Carroll et al. 2006, Ch. 4)? What specifically is new beyond applying this to the CATE-with-effect-modifier context? Please be precise.\n90\t\n91\t2. **Bayesian annotator model connection**: EC-HTE's hierarchical Dirichlet model for subgroup-specific confusion matrices is structurally similar to multi-annotator Bayesian models (Dawid & Skene 1979, Paun et al. 2018, Passonneau & Carpenter 2014). In those models, each annotator has their own confusion matrix with hierarchical shrinkage. Have you considered framing subgroups as \"annotators\" in this sense? Would existing Bayesian annotator model software (e.g., PyMC-based implementations) directly apply?\n92\t\n93\t3. **Multiple imputation baseline**: Have you considered or experimented with multiple imputation for misclassified categorical variables (Blackwell et al. 2017)? This is the most natural classical alternative and its absence from the comparison is notable.\n94\t\n95\t4. **\"Never worse than global\" qualification**: Appendix A shows EC-HTE is worse than global at K=8, n=50. The Limitations section mentions this, but the main text (Section 1, contribution #2) claims a \"never worse than global guarantee.\" Can you reconcile these statements?\n96\t\n97\t5. **Ashwin et al. reference verification**: The bibliography lists \"Ashwin, Maaz et al.\" but the paper_framing references \"Ashwin, Aditya et al.\" with a different title. Can you provide a stable URL or verify the correct citation?\n98\t\n99\t---\n100\t\n101\t## Novelty Assessment (Detailed)\n102\t\n103\t### Delta-pi x Delta-tau bias characterization\n104\t**Assessment: Moderately novel.** The qualitative insight (wrong correction can hurt) is not new in measurement error theory. The specific quantitative characterization (product form, sum-of-products for general K, R^2 = 0.982 empirical match) is new in the CATE-with-mismeasured-effect-modifier context. The contribution is a *formalization of a known-but-unrecognized-in-practice problem*, not a deep theoretical advance. For EMNLP, where the audience is primarily NLP/CSS rather than statistics, this level of novelty is acceptable.\n105\t\n106\t### EC-HTE framework\n107\t**Assessment: Incremental methodological novelty.** Hierarchical Bayesian Dirichlet models for confusion matrices are well-established. The application to subgroup-stratified CATE correction is sensible engineering, not a new technique. The integration with causal forests (pseudo-outcome approach) adds value. The \"never worse\" property via partial pooling is the key methodological insight, but it is a known property of shrinkage estimators. For EMNLP, this is acceptable as the method serves the problem identification.\n108\t\n109\t### Diagnostic criterion\n110\t**Assessment: Useful practical contribution.** AUC = 0.953 is strong. The standalone value -- practitioners can check their setup with a pilot study before deciding on correction strategy -- is genuine. This is a minor but clean contribution.\n111\t\n112\t### Overall novelty calibration\n113\tFor EMNLP 2026 (CL+CSS track), the novelty is borderline acceptable. The paper's strength is problem identification and comprehensive validation, not technical novelty. This is appropriate for the venue if the related work adequately contextualizes the contribution. Currently, the thin bibliography prevents this contextualization.\n114\t\n115\t---\n116\t\n117\t## Related Work Coverage Assessment\n118\t\n119\t| Area | Current coverage | What's missing | Priority |\n120\t|------|-----------------|----------------|----------|\n121\t| PPI/DSL | Adequate (3 refs) | PPI++, Zrnic & Candes 2024 | Medium |\n122\t| PPCI | Good (1 ref + experiment) | -- | OK |\n123\t| Classical meas. error | Thin (1 ref) | Gustafson 2003, Hu & Schennach 2008, Fuller 1987, Lewbel 2007 | **Critical** |\n124\t| Bayesian annotators | Thin (2 refs) | Passonneau & Carpenter 2014, Rodrigues & Pereira 2018, Hovy et al. 2013 | High |\n125\t| LLM-as-annotator CSS | **Zero refs** | Tornberg 2024, Gilardi et al. 2023, Pangakis et al. 2023, Ziems et al. 2024 | **Critical** |\n126\t| HTE methods | **Zero refs** | Wager & Athey 2018, Kennedy 2023, Kunzel et al. 2019, Chernozhukov et al. 2018 | **Critical** |\n127\t| Active annotation | Zero refs | Settles 2012, Zrnic & Candes 2024 | Low |\n128\t| Multiple imputation | Zero refs | Blackwell et al. 2017, Cole et al. 2006 | Medium |\n129\t\n130\tThe bibliography needs to approximately triple in size (from 9 to 25-35 references) to meet venue expectations. Three entire sub-literatures (LLM annotation in CSS, HTE methods, classical measurement error) have zero or one reference each. This is the single most urgent revision.\n131\t\n132\t---\n133\t\n134\t## Positioning and Framing Assessment\n135\t\n136\t**Venue fit**: EMNLP CL+CSS track is appropriate. The problem is grounded in NLP workflows (LLM annotation), the application domain is computational social science (political messaging, refugee interviews), and the method connects to the PPI/DSL ecosystem already published at NeurIPS/Science. A pure statistics journal would demand deeper theory; a pure ML venue might want broader method applicability beyond CSS.\n137\t\n138\t**\"Problem discovery\" framing**: Compelling and well-executed. The \"debiasing can hurt by 7.8x\" headline is striking and immediately actionable. The constructive example (Proposition 1) with policy-relevant implications (negative CATE for low-education group under global correction) grounds the problem concretely.\n139\t\n140\t**Overselling risk**: The \"never worse than global guarantee\" (contribution #2) is overstated given the K=8/n=50 counterexample. Otherwise, claims are well-calibrated to evidence.\n141\t\n142\t**Underselling risk**: The PPCI orthogonality + composition result (Section 5.5) is undersold. This is a genuine positive contribution -- showing that two correction methods for different error types compose cleanly with near-zero interaction -- and could be elevated in the abstract and contributions list.\n143\t\n144\t---\n145\t\n146\t## Impact Assessment\n147\t\n148\t**Primary audience**: CSS researchers using LLM annotations for causal analysis. This is a growing community (post-PPI, post-DSL), and the paper addresses a practical pitfall. Secondary audience: causal inference researchers studying measurement error propagation.\n149\t\n150\t**Adoption potential**: Moderate. The diagnostic criterion is immediately usable with zero implementation cost. EC-HTE requires MCMC (~2.3s per fit), which is practical but not trivial. The 250-label requirement is reasonable for studies already collecting some expert labels.\n151\t\n152\t**Research directions opened**: (1) Extension to continuous effect modifiers, (2) multi-LLM ensemble correction, (3) composition of covariate-side and outcome-side corrections as a general framework, (4) adaptive allocation strategies. The paper identifies but does not fully develop these.\n153\t\n154\t**Lasting impact**: Moderate risk that improving LLM accuracy reduces Delta-pi over time. However, the paper's own multi-LLM analysis (Appendix B) shows even the best LLM (Claude Sonnet 4.6, acc=0.89) still produces 4.1x amplification. Heterogeneity is structural (low-resource subgroups are systematically harder) and unlikely to vanish. For harder NLP tasks (sentiment intensity, stance detection, implicit framing), Delta-pi may increase.\n155\t\n156\t---\n157\t\n158\t## Writing Quality\n159\t\n160\tThe paper is clearly written with precise mathematical notation and well-organized experimental sections. The theoretical presentation (Section 3) is accessible to the CL+CSS audience. Tables are informative but too numerous -- consolidation and conversion to figures would improve readability. The abstract is strong and accurately represents the paper's contributions.\n161\t\n162\tOne organizational issue: the Diagnostic Criterion (Section 3.3) is wedged between Theory and Method, creating an odd flow. Consider moving it to after the EC-HTE framework description (as a \"when to use EC-HTE\" section) or into experiments.\n163\t\n164\t---\n165\t\n166\t## Scores\n167\t\n168\t| Dimension | Score | Notes |\n169\t|-----------|-------|-------|\n170\t| **Soundness** | 3 / 4 | Theory is clean for K=2, verified for K=4. MCMC diagnostics solid. \"Never worse\" claim overstated. Ashwin reference needs verification. |\n171\t| **Contribution** | 3 / 4 | Problem identification is novel and important. Method is competent but not deeply novel. Diagnostic is useful. Impact is moderate. |\n172\t| **Presentation** | 2.5 / 4 | Clear writing but too many tables (13 in main text), zero figures, thin bibliography. Density is high. |\n173\t| **Overall** | 4 / 6 | Weak Accept. Problem is real, formalization is clean, experiments are thorough. Bibliography gap is the main barrier to a confident accept. |\n174\t| **Confidence** | 4 / 5 | I am familiar with PPI, measurement error theory, Bayesian annotation models, and the CSS+LLM annotation ecosystem. |\n175\t\n176\t---\n177\t\n178\t## Revision Recommendations (Priority-Ordered)\n179\t\n180\t1. **[Critical] Expand bibliography to 25-35 references.** Add classical measurement error (Fuller, Gustafson, Hu & Schennach), LLM-as-annotator CSS (Tornberg, Gilardi, Pangakis, Ziems), HTE methods (Wager & Athey, Kennedy, Kunzel, Chernozhukov), and Bayesian annotator models (Passonneau & Carpenter, Hovy, Rodrigues). This is the single highest-priority revision.\n181\t\n182\t2. **[Critical] Explicitly contextualize against classical measurement error.** Address whether \"correction can amplify bias\" was already known (it was, qualitatively) and state what is specifically new (quantitative CATE product form, LLM-annotation workflow relevance, computable diagnostic).\n183\t\n184\t3. **[High] Add at least 2 figures to main text.** A bias mechanism schematic and a Delta-pi x Delta-tau amplification heatmap would replace 2000+ words of explanation. Consider also the diagnostic ROC curve.\n185\t\n186\t4. **[High] Qualify the \"never worse than global\" claim.** State conditions under which it holds (n_k >= ~30) and acknowledge the K=8/n=50 failure explicitly in the main text, not just appendix.\n187\t\n188\t5. **[Medium] Sharpen DSL distinction.** One crisp sentence in Related Work: \"DSL applies global correction because it targets ATE; when targeting subgroup-level CATEs with mismeasured effect modifiers, global correction introduces the bias mechanism we formalize in Theorem 1.\"\n189\t\n190\t6. **[Medium] Fix Ashwin et al. bibliography entry.** Verify author names and title; provide stable URL if available.\n191\t\n192\t7. **[Low] Consolidate tables.** Tables 1 and 2 (diagnostic and amplification) could merge. The appendix budget tables could merge. Target 8-10 main-text tables.\n193\t\n194\t---\n195\t\n196\t## Meta-Assessment\n197\t\n198\tThis paper improved substantially from Round 1: MCMC details, real-data validation, K>2 extension, and better density management in the main text. The core problem identification and the PPCI orthogonality demonstration are strong enough for EMNLP CL+CSS track. The remaining critical issue is bibliographic: the paper operates at the intersection of five literatures and cites only nine papers. Fixing this is straightforward and does not require new experiments. With an expanded bibliography, explicit measurement error contextualization, and 2-3 figures, this paper would be a solid accept.\n199\t\n200\t**Final Score: 4 / 6 (Weak Accept)**"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"243f7ab5-0b31-4460-862b-4acef4243b5d","uuid":"1df257f5-ba11-45a3-9fa7-19b62d477474","timestamp":"2026-05-20T20:22:06.534Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520/projects/ec_hte_llm_annotation/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T194535/best/review_report.md","content":"---\ndream_id: dream_20260520T194535\ntier: best\noverall_score: 4/6 Weak Accept\ntimestamp: 2026-05-20T20:21:49.054693+00:00\n---\n\n# Dream Review Report: \"When Debiasing Backfires: Measurement-Error Heterogeneity in LLM-Annotated Causal Effect Estimation\"\n\n**Review Mode**: Full (2 Reviewers, 4-step roundtable)\n**Date**: 2026-05-20 (Round 2)\n**Focus**: Novelty, Related Work, and Positioning (R2)\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 4 / 6 (Weak Accept)**\n\nThis paper identifies a genuine and well-formalized failure mode at the intersection of LLM annotation and heterogeneous treatment effect estimation. The core insight -- that global confusion matrix correction amplifies subgroup CATE bias in proportion to Delta-pi x Delta-tau -- is novel in its specific formalization and practically consequential. Compared to Round 1, the paper has materially improved: MCMC implementation details are now present (PyMC/NUTS, R-hat <= 1.004, ESS >= 387), the K>2 sum-of-products extension is explicitly stated and verified (Remark 1, Table 3), real-data validation on Ashwin et al. (2025) is included (Table 8-9), and secondary experiments have been moved to appendix, freeing space for deeper core discussion. However, the bibliography remains critically thin (9 references), novelty positioning still needs sharpening, and several related work gaps persist. These are fixable within one revision cycle.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nThe paper formalizes a bias mechanism in HTE estimation with LLM-annotated effect modifiers: applying a pooled confusion matrix to correct subgroup-level CATEs introduces bias proportional to the product of misclassification heterogeneity and CATE heterogeneity. The proposed EC-HTE framework uses hierarchical Bayesian Dirichlet partial pooling to estimate subgroup-stratified confusion matrices, correcting CATEs at the subgroup level. Experiments span synthetic parameter sweeps (R^2 = 0.982), semi-synthetic political tweet benchmarks with GPT-4.1 and Qwen3-235B (89-92% bias reduction), real refugee interview data from Ashwin et al. (6.7x amplification corrected), PPCI orthogonality demonstration, and causal forest integration. A diagnostic criterion (AUC = 0.953) predicts when global correction is harmful.\n\n---\n\n## Strengths\n\n**S1. Novel and well-scoped problem identification.** The specific failure mode -- that error correction *amplifies* bias when the mismeasured variable defines the heterogeneity structure -- has not been formalized before. The Delta-pi x Delta-tau product characterization (Theorem 1) is clean, intuitive, and verifiable. This is the paper's strongest contribution. While the underlying algebra is not deep, the *recognition* that this matters in the increasingly common LLM-annotation-for-CSS pipeline is genuinely new. Neither PPI (Angelopoulos et al. 2023), DSL (Egami et al. 2024), Mozer (2026), nor PPCI (Sui et al. 2026) address this case.\n\n**S2. Well-executed PPCI orthogonality demonstration (Section 5.5).** This is the paper's second-strongest contribution from a positioning standpoint. Rather than merely claiming EC-HTE differs from PPCI, the paper demonstrates empirically that (a) each method excels exclusively on its target error type, (b) PPCI actually *worsens* covariate-side error (bias 0.089 vs 0.021 uncorrected), and (c) their composition achieves the best result (bias 0.006). The near-zero correction-term correlation (r = 0.04) is convincing. This transforms a defensive distinction into a constructive contribution.\n\n**S3. Real-data validation now included.** The Ashwin et al. (2025) refugee interview results (Table 8-9) close the most critical gap from Round 1. The 6.7x amplification on education subgroups, producing a misleading *negative* CATE estimate for the low-education group, is compelling and policy-relevant. The cross-dimension consistency (4.2-6.7x amplification correlating with error ratio) is persuasive.\n\n**S4. MCMC implementation details now present.** Table 2 provides R-hat, ESS, divergence counts, and runtime. PyMC with NUTS, 4 chains, 1000 warmup. All diagnostics pass standard thresholds. This resolves the Round 1 reproducibility gap.\n\n**S5. Theory-experiment alignment improved.** Remark 1 explicitly extends the bias formula to general K via the sum-of-products form, with Table 3 providing K=4 verification (R^2 = 0.971). The theory-experiment gap from Round 1 is substantially narrowed.\n\n**S6. Honest and calibrated claims.** The paper correctly positions EC-HTE as \"problem identification + principled solution\" rather than a new estimation technique. Limitations are forthright about K=8 failure with 50 labels, computational overhead, and the a priori subgroup requirement. The \"never worse than global\" framing via partial pooling is stated as a practical property, not a theorem.\n\n**S7. Diagnostic criterion has genuine standalone value.** AUC = 0.953 with a stable threshold (0.008) across synthetic/semi-synthetic/real data gives practitioners a concrete decision rule from pilot annotations. Even researchers who do not adopt EC-HTE can use this diagnostic to assess whether their global correction is trustworthy.\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n### Major\n\n**W1. [Major] Bibliography remains critically thin (9 references).** This was the #1 structural issue flagged unanimously in Round 1, and it has not been addressed. For a paper bridging measurement error theory, Bayesian estimation, causal inference, LLM annotation, and computational social science, 9 references is far below the 30-40+ expected at EMNLP. Critical omissions include:\n\n- **Classical measurement error**: Gustafson (2003, *Measurement Error and Misclassification in Statistics and Epidemiology*); Hu & Schennach (2008) on semiparametric identification under misclassification; Battistin & Chesher (2014) on bunching with misclassification; Lewbel (2007) on endogenous regressor corrections. The paper cites only Carroll et al. (2006) -- a textbook -- without engaging the extensive subsequent literature on when misclassification correction can amplify MSE (a known phenomenon in the statistics literature since at least Fuller 1987).\n- **Bayesian annotator models**: Only Dawid & Skene (1979) and Paun et al. (2018) are cited. Missing: Passonneau & Carpenter (2014), Hovy et al. (2013), Rodrigues & Pereira (2018, learning from crowds with deep learning), Kim & Ghahramani (2012). The connection between EC-HTE's HB Dirichlet model and this literature is underexplored.\n- **LLM-as-annotator in CSS**: Tornberg (2024), Pangakis et al. (2023), Gilardi et al. (2023), Ziems et al. (2024) -- none cited. These papers document the heterogeneous error patterns that motivate EC-HTE.\n- **HTE estimation literature**: Wager & Athey (2018) for honest causal forests (used but not cited!), Kennedy (2023) for DR-learner, Kunzel et al. (2019) for X-learner, Nie & Wager (2021) for R-learner, Chernozhukov et al. (2018) for DML. The paper uses CausalForestDML without citing the foundational work.\n- **PPI extensions**: Angelopoulos et al. (2023b, PPI++), Zrnic & Candes (2024, active statistical inference). The broader PPI ecosystem is relevant.\n- **Multiple imputation for misclassified variables**: Blackwell et al. (2017), Cole et al. (2006) -- natural baselines that are neither cited nor compared against.\n\nThis omission is no longer merely a presentation issue; it actively undermines the novelty claim. A reader familiar with the measurement error literature may reasonably ask whether the \"debiasing can backfire\" insight was already known (and it partially was -- Fuller 1987 discusses variance inflation under misclassification correction). The paper needs to engage with this literature to establish what specifically is new here.\n\n**W2. [Major] Novelty positioning needs further sharpening against classical measurement error theory.** The core theoretical result -- that applying a global correction to heterogeneous subgroups introduces bias proportional to the deviation from the global error rate times the subgroup effect -- is a specific instantiation of a well-known phenomenon in the measurement error literature. Carroll et al. (2006, Ch. 4) discuss how misspecifying the measurement error distribution leads to biased corrections. The Delta-pi x Delta-tau product form is new in this specific CATE context, but the *qualitative insight* that applying the wrong correction can worsen estimates is not.\n\nThe paper should explicitly engage with this: \"It has long been known in the measurement error literature that misspecified corrections can amplify MSE (Fuller 1987, Sec. 2.3). Our contribution is (a) the specific quantitative characterization for CATE estimation when the mismeasured variable defines the heterogeneity structure, (b) demonstrating that this occurs by default in the LLM-annotation workflow where global confusion matrices are standard practice, and (c) providing a computable diagnostic and correction framework.\" Without this contextualization, a statistics-trained reviewer may perceive the theoretical contribution as trivially derivable.\n\n**W3. [Major] The Ashwin et al. (2025) reference appears problematic.** The bibliography entry reads \"Ashwin, Maaz et al. 2025. Differential LLM annotation errors across demographic subgroups in refugee interview data. Working paper.\" But in the paper_framing.md the authors are listed as \"Ashwin, Aditya; Swanson, Becca; Blair, Robert; Samii, Cyrus\" and the year is 2025 with a different title (\"Using Large Language Models for Qualitative Analysis can Introduce Serious Bias\"). The main text says \"Ashwin et al. (2025)\" for the refugee data. The bibliography entry has incorrect first author name (\"Maaz\" vs \"Aditya\") and a different paper title than what the framing document describes. This needs to be verified and corrected. If this is a working paper without a stable URL, its verifiability as ground-truth evidence is weakened.\n\n### Moderate\n\n**W4. [Moderate] Missing natural baselines.** SIMEX (Simulation-Extrapolation, Cook & Stefanski 1994) and multiple imputation for misclassified categorical variables (Blackwell et al. 2017) are standard measurement error correction baselines absent from the experiments. The paper compares EC-HTE against no correction, global correction, MLE stratified, and PPCI, but omits the most obvious classical alternatives. Even if these methods are expected to perform poorly (they likely would, because they don't stratify by subgroup), their absence leaves a gap in the experimental narrative.\n\n**W5. [Moderate] DSL distinction remains insufficiently crisp.** Egami et al. (2024) DSL is a direct competitor for the CSS+LLM audience. Both EC-HTE and DSL use confusion matrix estimation. The key distinction -- DSL applies a global correction appropriate for ATE because it targets population-level estimands, while EC-HTE needs subgroup-specific corrections because the mismeasured variable defines the conditioning structure for HTE -- should be stated in a single, prominent sentence. Currently the reader must infer this from the Related Work paragraph.\n\n**W6. [Moderate] Invertibility assumption unstated.** Equation (3) applies C_k^{-1}. For binary X, this requires det(C_k) != 0, i.e., the classifier must beat random chance per subgroup. For small K and small n_k, some subgroup confusion matrices may be near-singular, especially under the HB prior at small n. This assumption should be stated, and the HB prior's role in preventing near-singularity should be discussed.\n\n**W7. [Moderate] \"Never worse than global\" claim is informal.** The paper states this as a property of partial pooling but never formally proves it. In the extreme case (K=8, n=50), EC-HTE is worse than global (Appendix A, Table A.3). The claim should be qualified: \"Under reasonable per-subgroup sample sizes (n_k >= 30), EC-HTE's partial pooling guarantees non-inferior performance to global correction.\" As stated, it is contradicted by the paper's own robustness results.\n\n### Minor\n\n**W8. [Minor] Semi-synthetic ground truth design.** The semi-synthetic setup injects a known CATE structure into real tweet data. The resulting \"ground truth\" CATEs are by construction perfectly aligned with the binary subgroup structure. In practice, subgroup boundaries are fuzzy, and CATE heterogeneity may not partition cleanly. The robustness analysis (Appendix C) partially addresses this, but the main semi-synthetic results may overstate EC-HTE's advantage relative to noisier real-world settings.\n\n**W9. [Minor] No figures in main text.** While tables are informative, key patterns -- the bias amplification heatmap (Delta-pi vs Delta-tau), the diagnostic ROC curve, the bias mechanism schematic -- would be far more effective as figures. The paper's 13 tables in 8 pages of main text (plus 4 appendix tables) make it read more as a technical report than a conference paper. At minimum, a mechanism schematic and the amplification heatmap should be in the main text.\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Classical measurement error context**: How does Theorem 1 relate to the well-known result that misspecified measurement error corrections can increase MSE (Fuller 1987, Sec. 2.3; Carroll et al. 2006, Ch. 4)? What specifically is new beyond applying this to the CATE-with-effect-modifier context? Please be precise.\n\n2. **Bayesian annotator model connection**: EC-HTE's hierarchical Dirichlet model for subgroup-specific confusion matrices is structurally similar to multi-annotator Bayesian models (Dawid & Skene 1979, Paun et al. 2018, Passonneau & Carpenter 2014). In those models, each annotator has their own confusion matrix with hierarchical shrinkage. Have you considered framing subgroups as \"annotators\" in this sense? Would existing Bayesian annotator model software (e.g., PyMC-based implementations) directly apply?\n\n3. **Multiple imputation baseline**: Have you considered or experimented with multiple imputation for misclassified categorical variables (Blackwell et al. 2017)? This is the most natural classical alternative and its absence from the comparison is notable.\n\n4. **\"Never worse than global\" qualification**: Appendix A shows EC-HTE is worse than global at K=8, n=50. The Limitations section mentions this, but the main text (Section 1, contribution #2) claims a \"never worse than global guarantee.\" Can you reconcile these statements?\n\n5. **Ashwin et al. reference verification**: The bibliography lists \"Ashwin, Maaz et al.\" but the paper_framing references \"Ashwin, Aditya et al.\" with a different title. Can you provide a stable URL or verify the correct citation?\n\n---\n\n## Novelty Assessment (Detailed)\n\n### Delta-pi x Delta-tau bias characterization\n**Assessment: Moderately novel.** The qualitative insight (wrong correction can hurt) is not new in measurement error theory. The specific quantitative characterization (product form, sum-of-products for general K, R^2 = 0.982 empirical match) is new in the CATE-with-mismeasured-effect-modifier context. The contribution is a *formalization of a known-but-unrecognized-in-practice problem*, not a deep theoretical advance. For EMNLP, where the audience is primarily NLP/CSS rather than statistics, this level of novelty is acceptable.\n\n### EC-HTE framework\n**Assessment: Incremental methodological novelty.** Hierarchical Bayesian Dirichlet models for confusion matrices are well-established. The application to subgroup-stratified CATE correction is sensible engineering, not a new technique. The integration with causal forests (pseudo-outcome approach) adds value. The \"never worse\" property via partial pooling is the key methodological insight, but it is a known property of shrinkage estimators. For EMNLP, this is acceptable as the method serves the problem identification.\n\n### Diagnostic criterion\n**Assessment: Useful practical contribution.** AUC = 0.953 is strong. The standalone value -- practitioners can check their setup with a pilot study before deciding on correction strategy -- is genuine. This is a minor but clean contribution.\n\n### Overall novelty calibration\nFor EMNLP 2026 (CL+CSS track), the novelty is borderline acceptable. The paper's strength is problem identification and comprehensive validation, not technical novelty. This is appropriate for the venue if the related work adequately contextualizes the contribution. Currently, the thin bibliography prevents this contextualization.\n\n---\n\n## Related Work Coverage Assessment\n\n| Area | Current coverage | What's missing | Priority |\n|------|-----------------|----------------|----------|\n| PPI/DSL | Adequate (3 refs) | PPI++, Zrnic & Candes 2024 | Medium |\n| PPCI | Good (1 ref + experiment) | -- | OK |\n| Classical meas. error | Thin (1 ref) | Gustafson 2003, Hu & Schennach 2008, Fuller 1987, Lewbel 2007 | **Critical** |\n| Bayesian annotators | Thin (2 refs) | Passonneau & Carpenter 2014, Rodrigues & Pereira 2018, Hovy et al. 2013 | High |\n| LLM-as-annotator CSS | **Zero refs** | Tornberg 2024, Gilardi et al. 2023, Pangakis et al. 2023, Ziems et al. 2024 | **Critical** |\n| HTE methods | **Zero refs** | Wager & Athey 2018, Kennedy 2023, Kunzel et al. 2019, Chernozhukov et al. 2018 | **Critical** |\n| Active annotation | Zero refs | Settles 2012, Zrnic & Candes 2024 | Low |\n| Multiple imputation | Zero refs | Blackwell et al. 2017, Cole et al. 2006 | Medium |\n\nThe bibliography needs to approximately triple in size (from 9 to 25-35 references) to meet venue expectations. Three entire sub-literatures (LLM annotation in CSS, HTE methods, classical measurement error) have zero or one reference each. This is the single most urgent revision.\n\n---\n\n## Positioning and Framing Assessment\n\n**Venue fit**: EMNLP CL+CSS track is appropriate. The problem is grounded in NLP workflows (LLM annotation), the application domain is computational social science (political messaging, refugee interviews), and the method connects to the PPI/DSL ecosystem already published at NeurIPS/Science. A pure statistics journal would demand deeper theory; a pure ML venue might want broader method applicability beyond CSS.\n\n**\"Problem discovery\" framing**: Compelling and well-executed. The \"debiasing can hurt by 7.8x\" headline is striking and immediately actionable. The constructive example (Proposition 1) with policy-relevant implications (negative CATE for low-education group under global correction) grounds the problem concretely.\n\n**Overselling risk**: The \"never worse than global guarantee\" (contribution #2) is overstated given the K=8/n=50 counterexample. Otherwise, claims are well-calibrated to evidence.\n\n**Underselling risk**: The PPCI orthogonality + composition result (Section 5.5) is undersold. This is a genuine positive contribution -- showing that two correction methods for different error types compose cleanly with near-zero interaction -- and could be elevated in the abstract and contributions list.\n\n---\n\n## Impact Assessment\n\n**Primary audience**: CSS researchers using LLM annotations for causal analysis. This is a growing community (post-PPI, post-DSL), and the paper addresses a practical pitfall. Secondary audience: causal inference researchers studying measurement error propagation.\n\n**Adoption potential**: Moderate. The diagnostic criterion is immediately usable with zero implementation cost. EC-HTE requires MCMC (~2.3s per fit), which is practical but not trivial. The 250-label requirement is reasonable for studies already collecting some expert labels.\n\n**Research directions opened**: (1) Extension to continuous effect modifiers, (2) multi-LLM ensemble correction, (3) composition of covariate-side and outcome-side corrections as a general framework, (4) adaptive allocation strategies. The paper identifies but does not fully develop these.\n\n**Lasting impact**: Moderate risk that improving LLM accuracy reduces Delta-pi over time. However, the paper's own multi-LLM analysis (Appendix B) shows even the best LLM (Claude Sonnet 4.6, acc=0.89) still produces 4.1x amplification. Heterogeneity is structural (low-resource subgroups are systematically harder) and unlikely to vanish. For harder NLP tasks (sentiment intensity, stance detection, implicit framing), Delta-pi may increase.\n\n---\n\n## Writing Quality\n\nThe paper is clearly written with precise mathematical notation and well-organized experimental sections. The theoretical presentation (Section 3) is accessible to the CL+CSS audience. Tables are informative but too numerous -- consolidation and conversion to figures would improve readability. The abstract is strong and accurately represents the paper's contributions.\n\nOne organizational issue: the Diagnostic Criterion (Section 3.3) is wedged between Theory and Method, creating an odd flow. Consider moving it to after the EC-HTE framework description (as a \"when to use EC-HTE\" section) or into experiments.\n\n---\n\n## Scores\n\n| Dimension | Score | Notes |\n|-----------|-------|-------|\n| **Soundness** | 3 / 4 | Theory is clean for K=2, verified for K=4. MCMC diagnostics solid. \"Never worse\" claim overstated. Ashwin reference needs verification. |\n| **Contribution** | 3 / 4 | Problem identification is novel and important. Method is competent but not deeply novel. Diagnostic is useful. Impact is moderate. |\n| **Presentation** | 2.5 / 4 | Clear writing but too many tables (13 in main text), zero figures, thin bibliography. Density is high. |\n| **Overall** | 4 / 6 | Weak Accept. Problem is real, formalization is clean, experiments are thorough. Bibliography gap is the main barrier to a confident accept. |\n| **Confidence** | 4 / 5 | I am familiar with PPI, measurement error theory, Bayesian annotation models, and the CSS+LLM annotation ecosystem. |\n\n---\n\n## Revision Recommendations (Priority-Ordered)\n\n1. **[Critical] Expand bibliography to 25-35 references.** Add classical measurement error (Fuller, Gustafson, Hu & Schennach), LLM-as-annotator CSS (Tornberg, Gilardi, Pangakis, Ziems), HTE methods (Wager & Athey, Kennedy, Kunzel, Chernozhukov), and Bayesian annotator models (Passonneau & Carpenter, Hovy, Rodrigues). This is the single highest-priority revision.\n\n2. **[Critical] Explicitly contextualize against classical measurement error.** Address whether \"correction can amplify bias\" was already known (it was, qualitatively) and state what is specifically new (quantitative CATE product form, LLM-annotation workflow relevance, computable diagnostic).\n\n3. **[High] Add at least 2 figures to main text.** A bias mechanism schematic and a Delta-pi x Delta-tau amplification heatmap would replace 2000+ words of explanation. Consider also the diagnostic ROC curve.\n\n4. **[High] Qualify the \"never worse than global\" claim.** State conditions under which it holds (n_k >= ~30) and acknowledge the K=8/n=50 failure explicitly in the main text, not just appendix.\n\n5. **[Medium] Sharpen DSL distinction.** One crisp sentence in Related Work: \"DSL applies global correction because it targets ATE; when targeting subgroup-level CATEs with mismeasured effect modifiers, global correction introduces the bias mechanism we formalize in Theorem 1.\"\n\n6. **[Medium] Fix Ashwin et al. bibliography entry.** Verify author names and title; provide stable URL if available.\n\n7. **[Low] Consolidate tables.** Tables 1 and 2 (diagnostic and amplification) could merge. The appendix budget tables could merge. Target 8-10 main-text tables.\n\n---\n\n## Meta-Assessment\n\nThis paper improved substantially from Round 1: MCMC details, real-data validation, K>2 extension, and better density management in the main text. The core problem identification and the PPCI orthogonality demonstration are strong enough for EMNLP CL+CSS track. The remaining critical issue is bibliographic: the paper operates at the intersection of five literatures and cites only nine papers. Fixing this is straightforward and does not require new experiments. With an expanded bibliography, explicit measurement error contextualization, and 2-3 figures, this paper would be a solid accept.\n\n**Final Score: 4 / 6 (Weak Accept)**","numLines":200,"startLine":1,"totalLines":200}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01D6gN4gLTQ5K2ZafnZNtpwv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I see that R2's review was written to the file, but R1's review was apparently overwritten by R2's. Let me check if R1's full review was captured in the agent summary. From the R1 agent output, I got a summary but not the full review text. The file currently contains only R2's review.\n\nLet me reconstruct R1's review from the summary provided:\n- Score: 4.0/6 (Weak Accept)\n- 4 major weaknesses, 5 minor weaknesses, 5 key strengths\n- Major: (1) Theorem 1 is identity not bound, (2) Semi-synthetic DGP underspecified, (3) Real-data ground truth opaque, (4) Diagnostic AUC on development data\n\n\n- Key strengths: Clean problem identification, comprehensive 11-experiment coverage, well-motivated HB estimation, convincing PPCI orthogonality, honest failure mode reporting\n\nNow I have both reviews and can move forward with the roundtable discussion. Both reviewers independently arrived at 4/6 (Weak Accept), which is a strong convergence point. I need to identify where they align and where they diverge before synthesizing a final combined report.\n\nLooking at the convergences: both recognize the novel problem framing and importance, both appreciate the PPCI orthogonality argument, both value the real-data validation, and both acknowledge the honest treatment of limitations. The key technical disagreement is that R1 views Theorem 1 as an identity rather than a bound, while R2 sees the novelty as moderate given existing measurement error literature.\n\nThe divergences are instructive—R1 critiques the methodological foundations (DGP specification, ground truth opacity), while R2 focuses on positioning and bibliographic gaps. R1 also flags that the diagnostic AUC wasn't evaluated on held-out data, and R2 questions whether the \"never worse than global\" claim is overstated.\n\nNow I'll construct the full roundtable synthesis and prepare the final combined report.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6958,"cache_read_input_tokens":67083,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6958},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"243f7ab5-0b31-4460-862b-4acef4243b5d","uuid":"fcc0d427-1d6b-4105-845c-80e5f21bc5b5"}
